-- title:
--   Proposition 4.5 — the associated graphical game of a smooth (resp. hyperfinite) Bayesian game is smooth (resp. hyperfinite)
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a purely atomic Bayesian game on a standard Borel state space with finite non-empty player set $I$, and let $G_T$ be the graph of its associated graphical game on the type space $\Omega_T=\bigsqcup_i T^i$ (types $\tau,\tau'$ are adjacent when they are distinct and realized at a common state). Then
--
--   1. $G_T$ is a Borel graph of countable degree;
--   2. if the common knowledge relation of $B$ is smooth, the connected-component relation of $G_T$ is smooth, i.e. $G_T$ is a smooth graph;
--   3. if the common knowledge relation of $B$ is hyperfinite, $G_T$ is a hyperfinite graph.
--
--   Thus the passage from a Bayesian game to its agent-normal-form graphical game keeps the game on the same rung of the CBER hierarchy; this is the first step of the proof of the main theorem.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Only the graph matters, so no common action set is assumed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: Ω a Polish space with its Borel σ-algebra → Ω a standard Borel space; reason: every Polish space with its Borel σ-algebra is standard Borel, and every notion in the statement depends only on the measurable structure, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: "the induced graphical game is smooth (resp. hyperfinite)" for a smooth (resp. hyperfinite) game → in addition, G_T is a Borel graph of countable degree for every purely atomic Bayesian game; reason: the paper asserts this in Definition 3.3 and §4.2 ("straightforwardly satisfies the conditions"); the smooth and hyperfinite clauses are as printed
--   - encoding: a purely atomic Bayesian game (Ω, I, t, (µⁱ), A, r) with knowledge relations Eᵢ → the structure BayesianGame: Eᵢ is tⁱ_x = tⁱ_y, purely atomic is countability of its classes, the prior condition (2.2) is (µⁱ).bind tⁱ = µⁱ, payoffs are given on pure profiles; reason: the paper defines Eᵢ from the types (p.11); the second clause of (2.2) and the purely atomic types of Definition 2.9 follow from the field `knows`
--   - encoding: Ω_T = ⊔ᵢ Ω/tⁱ ≅ ⊔ᵢ Image(tⁱ), u_T(τⁱ, σ) = rⁱ(σ | τⁱ), µ_T (4.5), the induced strategies → TypeSpace B = Σ i, range (tⁱ); u_T on pure profiles as ∫ rⁱ(ω, (a(tʲ(ω)))ⱼ) dτ(ω), extended by the product measure; µ_T = |I|⁻² Σ_{i,j} (typeOf i)_*(µʲ); σⁱ(ω) = σ_T(tⁱ(ω)); reason: the same objects; the mixed extension of u_T equals rⁱ(σ | τ) because the vertices tʲ(ω), j ∈ I, are distinct
--   - encoding: smooth: a Borel ψ into some Polish space Z with x E y ⟺ ψ(x) = ψ(y) (Definition 2.1) → a measurable ψ : X → ℝ with the same property; reason: every Polish space is Borel isomorphic to a Borel subset of ℝ; the paper's §6.1 states smoothness as E ≤_B id_ℝ
--   - encoding: hyperfinite: generated by a Borel ℤ-action, or alternatively an increasing union of Borel finite equivalence relations (§2.3) → the increasing-union form; reason: the paper states the two forms as equivalent for CBERs (Dougherty–Jackson–Kechris 1994) and its proofs use the increasing-union form
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.5, p.21; §4.2, p.20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Bayes_ckRel
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Bayes_assocGraph
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsSmoothGraph
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsHyperfiniteGraph

namespace HellmanLevy.Bayes

/-- **Proposition 4.5.** If a Bayesian game is smooth (resp. hyperfinite), then its associated
graphical game is smooth (resp. hyperfinite): the graph `G_T` on the type space `Ω_T` is a Borel
graph of countable degree whose connected-component relation is smooth (resp. hyperfinite).
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.5, p.21; the associated game is defined in §4.2, p.20.

**Formalization Note** The statement concerns only the graph `G_T`, so no common action set is
needed. The first clause (`G_T` is a Borel graph of countable degree) is asserted by the paper
in Definition 3.3 and §4.2 ("straightforwardly satisfies"); it is stated unconditionally. -/
theorem proposition_4_5 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] [StandardBorelSpace Ω] {I : Type*}
    [Fintype I] [Nonempty I] {A : I → Type*} (B : BayesianGame Ω I A) :
    Graphical.IsGraph (assocGraph B) ∧
      (CBER.IsSmooth (ckRel B) → Graphical.IsSmoothGraph (assocGraph B)) ∧
      (CBER.IsHyperfinite (ckRel B) → Graphical.IsHyperfiniteGraph (assocGraph B)) := by sorry

end HellmanLevy.Bayes
